narrative:
You beat him by a stride, maybe less, and he collapses against the well laughing too hard to be a good loser about it.

@Denn: Nobody's done that in - actually, nobody's done that. Ever. I need a name to put on the loss so I can plan my revenge properly.

He means it as a joke. He also means it completely.

He beats you to the well by enough distance that there's no pretending otherwise, and turns around grinning, not even winded.

@Denn: Gracious in defeat. I respect that. Wish I saw it more often, honestly - most people just accuse me of cheating, which, to be fair, I have done, but not this time.

He offers a hand up you didn't strictly need but take anyway.

@You: Buy your own first round. I didn't agree to anything.

@Denn: Nobody ever does! That's what makes it a challenge and not a contract. Suit yourself, stranger - the offer stands whenever you change your mind.

He peels off toward the well anyway, in no real hurry now that there's no one to race.

Something is sitting cross-legged on the fence post at the village edge that you'd swear wasn't there a moment ago - patched clothes in no pattern that matches any village you've passed through, mismatched boots, a grin that arrives before any reason for one.

@Piebald Fool: -so I told the crow, the road never lies about the first thing it carried, only the last. You weren't listening. Were you listening?

It's unclear whether the conversation started before you arrived, or whether it started the moment you did.

A cart loaded with more goods than should physically fit sits pulled off at the square's edge. A wiry old man perches on the tailgate, watching you approach with the frank appraisal of someone doing math.

@Ferrick: That's a few days of dust on you. Costs nothing to look. Costs a story or a coin to leave with something.

He pats the tailgate beside him. _Everything on this cart has a price. Some of it isn't coin._

He doesn't draw a weapon. He doesn't need to - he simply doesn't move, and the road behind him stays exactly as blocked as it was, and the two of you stand there long enough for the standoff itself to become the whole conversation.

@Tollkeeper: You can stand there as long as you like. I have considerably more patience for this than you do. I recommend making your peace with that before the light goes.

Eventually, it's you who breaks first - not from fear, just from the plain arithmetic of a road you actually need to be on.

A woman is sitting very still at the next crossroads marker, in the way of someone who has been waiting exactly as long as she intended to and not a moment longer.

@Saoirse: You've been walking since before the light went. Most people stop when they're tired. Why haven't you?

She doesn't introduce herself. She doesn't need to yet - the question matters more than her name does, for now.

It takes longer than you expected, and she makes you repeat things twice, three times, until reading the signs stops being something she's telling you and starts being something you'd notice on your own.

@Saoirse: There. Now you'll see it coming, half the time, instead of finding out the way most people do - after.

She says it like a small thing. It doesn't feel like a small thing.

You nod along at the right moments, but half of it slides past you, and she notices - of course she notices, that's the entire point of her.

@Saoirse: You're not listening. That's your business, not mine. Just don't come find me later surprised by something I already showed you once.

She doesn't repeat herself.
